{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2124 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "cfcf8cdf0b3dcc98fa49407b064c6e9a51607141ab38fa45e911f4d857ef0b8c" :edb/db-before-t 2123 :edb/db-after-t 2124 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535436 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:03:42.987Z" 13194139535436 true] [17592186078252 1100 "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle 2t, 3t^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,2)$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186078252 1100 "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle 2t, 3t^2\\rangle$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,2)$ when $t=0$, calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t$." 13194139535436 false] [17592186078252 1145 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨2t, 3t^{2}⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫2tdt, ∫3t^{2}dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{2} + C_{1}, t^{3} + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 1,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨(0)^{2} + C_{1}, (0)^{3} + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨1, 2⟩ &= ⟨C_{1}, C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=1$ and $C_2=2$. Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ⟨t^{2} + 1, t^{3} + 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535436 false] [17592186078252 1145 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t,3{t}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 1,2 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,(0)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=1$ and $C_2=2.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}+1,\\,{t}^{3}+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186078253 1100 "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $\\mathbf{r}=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0\\right\\rangle$ when $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}2,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\pi.$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186078253 1100 "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t\\rangle$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $\\mathbf{r}=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0\\right\\rangle$ when $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}2$, calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\pi$." 13194139535436 false] [17592186078253 1145 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨\\cos t, \\sin t⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫\\cos tdt, ∫\\sin tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\sin t + C_{1},-\\cos t + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\langle 0,0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ⟨\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) + C_{1},-\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨0, 0⟩ &= ⟨1 + C_{1}, 0 + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2 = 0$. Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ⟨\\sin t - 1,-\\cos t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the position at time $t=\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(π) &= ⟨\\sin π - 1,-\\cos π⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0 - 1, - (-1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535436 false] [17592186078253 1145 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r}\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,0+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the position at time $t=\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-1,\\,-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186078254 1100 "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle 2t, 1 \\rangle\\,,\\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $\\mathbf{r} = \\left\\langle -1,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times at which the particle is at the origin." 13194139535436 true] [17592186078254 1100 "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle 2t, 1 \\rangle\\,,\\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $\\mathbf{r} = \\left\\langle -1,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0$, calculate the times at which the particle is at the origin." 13194139535436 false] [17592186078254 1145 "The particle is at the origin when its position is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\left< 0,0 \\right>\n$$\n\nSo first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨2t, 1⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫2tdt, ∫1dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{2} + C_{1}, t + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle -1, \\, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨(0)^{2} + C_{1}, (0) + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨ - 1, 1⟩ &= ⟨C_{1}, C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2=1$. Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ⟨t^{2} - 1, t + 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve for the times at which the position is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= 0 \\\\\n⟨t^{2} - 1, t + 1⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - 1 &= 0⇒t = ± 1, \\\\\nt + 1 &= 0⇒t =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t= -1$, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is at the origin." 13194139535436 false] [17592186078254 1145 "The particle is at the origin when its position is $\\mathbf{r} = \\left< 0,0 \\right>.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t,1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle -1, \\, 1 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,\\,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2=1.$ Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}-1,\\,t+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve for the times at which the position is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{2}-1,\\,t+1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ t+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t= -1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is at the origin." 13194139535436 true] [17592186078277 1100 "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) =  \\left<   9\\cos {3t},\\, \\sin{t} \\right>.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $\\left\\langle 2,\\,0  \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186078277 1100 "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\left< 9\\cos {3t},\\, \\sin{t} \\right>$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $\\left\\langle 2,\\,0 \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0$, calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t$." 13194139535436 false] [17592186078277 1145 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨9\\cos 3t, \\sin t⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫9\\cos 3tdt, ∫\\sin tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\sin 3t + C_{1},-\\cos t + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} (0)= \\langle 2,\\, 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(0) &= ⟨3\\sin 0 + C_{1},-\\cos 0 + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨2, 0⟩ &= ⟨C_{1},-1 + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=2$ and $C_2=1$. Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\left< 3\\sin{3t} +2, \\, 1-\\cos{t} \\right>\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨3\\sin 3t + 2, 1 - \\cos t⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫3\\sin 3t + 2dt, ∫1 - \\cos tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\cos 3t + 2t + C_{3}, t - \\sin t + C_{4}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 0,0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨ - \\cos 0 + 2(0) + C_{3}, 0 - \\sin 0 + C_{4}⟩ \\\\\n⟨0, 0⟩ &= ⟨ - 1 + C_{3}, C_{4}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_3=1$ and $C_4=0$. Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< -\\cos {3t} + 2t + 1, \\, t-\\sin{t} \\right>\n$$" 13194139535436 false] [17592186078277 1145 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v} (0)= \\langle 2,\\, 0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,\\,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=2$ and $C_2=1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\left<      3\\sin{3t} +2 , \\,    1-\\cos{t} \\right>.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2t+{C}_{3},\\,t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(0)+{C}_{3},\\,0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{3},\\,{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_3=1$ and $C_4=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left<  -\\cos {3t} + 2t + 1 , \\,   t-\\sin{t}  \\right>.\n$$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186078402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349035 13194139535436 true] [17592186078402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349062 13194139535436 true] [17592186078402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349065 13194139535436 true] [17592186078402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349100 13194139535436 true] [17592186078402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349107 13194139535436 true] [17592186078403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349042 13194139535436 true] [17592186078403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349069 13194139535436 true] [17592186078403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349074 13194139535436 true] [17592186078403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349121 13194139535436 true] [17592186078403 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349128 13194139535436 true] [17592186078404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349056 13194139535436 true] [17592186078404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349080 13194139535436 true] [17592186078404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349083 13194139535436 true] [17592186078404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349089 13194139535436 true] [17592186078404 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349135 13194139535436 true] [17592186078423 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349049 13194139535436 true] [17592186078423 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349094 13194139535436 true] [17592186078423 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349097 13194139535436 true] [17592186078423 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349114 13194139535436 true] [17592186079111 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535436 true] [17592186079111 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨6t-2,4⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $⟨1,\\,4⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186079111 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$a(t) = ⟨6t - 2, 4⟩$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0, 0)$ and has velocity $⟨1, 4⟩$ when $t = 1$, calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t$." 13194139535436 false] [17592186079111 1145 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t-2,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (6t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2t+{C}_{1},\\,4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,4⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨3(1)^{2}-2(1)+{C}_{1},\\,4(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,4⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,4t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+{C}_{3},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}+{C}_{3},\\,2(1)^{2}+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},2+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=0$ and ${C}_{4}=-2.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349035 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349036 13194139535436 true] [17592186349035 1089 "q-122422" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349035 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535436 true] [17592186349035 1100 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,-1⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(5,2)$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349035 1113 #uuid "a1710a71-bffa-5466-ace2-803c1c69d643" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349035 1145 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\sqrt{t+1}+{C}_{1},\\,-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨5,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨2\\sqrt{3+1}+{C}_{1},\\,-3+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5,2⟩ & =⟨4+{C}_{1},-3+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t+1}+1,\\,5-t⟩.\n$$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349037 13194139535436 true] [17592186349036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349038 13194139535436 true] [17592186349036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349039 13194139535436 true] [17592186349036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349040 13194139535436 true] [17592186349036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349041 13194139535436 true] [1759218634