ector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t},-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{C}_{3},-t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨2,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=2$ and ${C}_{4}=4.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349051 13194139535436 true] [17592186349050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349052 13194139535436 true] [17592186349050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349053 13194139535436 true] [17592186349050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349054 13194139535436 true] [17592186349050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349055 13194139535436 true] [17592186349050 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535436 true] [17592186349050 1041 "selection" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349050 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349053 13194139535436 true] [17592186349050 1110 #uuid "181157cd-abf4-5ad5-9ee9-04982fdc6344" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349051 1034 "r(t)=⟨2+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,3t⟩" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349051 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535436 true] [17592186349051 1111 #uuid "95a6873d-64d5-5e73-aec0-04eaf6796563" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349052 1034 "r(t)=⟨2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,5t-2⟩" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349052 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535436 true] [17592186349052 1111 #uuid "a788fddf-c310-54e0-9f37-16b9ddcafe13" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349053 1034 "r(t)=⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349053 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535436 true] [17592186349053 1111 #uuid "f6d7b9c4-95eb-55ab-8250-1187a92e9b23" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349054 1034 "r(t)=⟨2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535436 true] [17592186349054 1111 #uuid "73eaeed2-bee2-58b2-bf3c-4d8b8a56eaa7" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349055 1034 "r(t)=⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,5t-2⟩" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535436 true] [17592186349055 1111 #uuid "0f77b51b-c52a-53ea-bb16-11d1b5d4a554" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349056 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349057 13194139535436 true] [17592186349056 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349060 13194139535436 true] [17592186349056 1089 "q-347213" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349056 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535436 true] [17592186349056 1100 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t),\\,2t-5⟩,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨0,6⟩$ when $t=0,$ find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535436 true] [17592186349056 1113 #uuid "4f05e01d-af4a-515f-9c14-f25f5b6cb698" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349056 1145 "The particle intersects the $x$-axis when its position is $r=⟨x,0⟩,$ where $x$ is arbitrary. So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t),\\,2t-5⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (2t-5)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}-5t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{C}_{1},\\,{0}^{2}-5(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,6⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=6.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t),\\,{t}^{2}-5t+6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times  when the particle intersects the $x$-axis, we solve for the times at which the $y$-coordinate of the position vector is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-5t+6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t-3) & =0 \\\\ t & =2,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=2$ and $t=3.$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349058 13194139535436 true] [17592186349057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349059 13194139535436 true] [17592186349057 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535436 true] [17592186349057 1041 "field-1" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349057 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349058 13194139535436 true] [17592186349057 1110 #uuid "c7f746c0-59c5-5c99-a767-9aebec0268b8" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349058 1034 "2" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349058 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535436 true] [17592186349058 1111 #uuid "5c2a3f9d-6752-5cad-8ca7-8486a8f0ee59" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349059 1034 "1" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535436 true] [17592186349059 1111 #uuid "c4cadf28-f197-5656-a291-b012a1177a6b" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349060 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349061 13194139535436 true] [17592186349060 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535436 true] [17592186349060 1041 "field-2" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349060 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349061 13194139535436 true] [17592186349060 1110 #uuid "523314ae-6e80-5e67-a1fd-1a860b120837" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349061 1034 "3" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349061 1103 #edb/ref 175921