 solve for the times at which the $y$-coordinate of the position vector is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(t-1)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =1,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t$ must be positive, we conclude that the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=1.$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349081 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349082 13194139535436 true] [17592186349081 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535436 true] [17592186349081 1041 "field-1" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349081 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349082 13194139535436 true] [17592186349081 1110 #uuid "7c8b9856-18c8-5a0f-8443-402649f39d52" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349082 1034 "1" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349082 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535436 true] [17592186349082 1111 #uuid "e05f67ff-37c6-55a2-b19e-ba7132d07df5" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349083 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349084 13194139535436 true] [17592186349083 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349087 13194139535436 true] [17592186349083 1089 "q-351258" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349083 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535436 true] [17592186349083 1100 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t+1},\\,2t-5⟩,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨0,6⟩$ when $t=0,$ find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535436 true] [17592186349083 1113 #uuid "1a43feef-aede-592a-b49e-54c3a6504c69" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349083 1145 "The particle intersects the $x$-axis when its position is $r=⟨x,0⟩,$ where $x$ is arbitrary. So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{2}{t+1},\\,2t-5⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{2}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (2t-5)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}-5t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+{C}_{1},\\,{0}^{2}-5(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,6⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=6.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,{t}^{2}-5t+6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis, we solve for the times at which the $y$-coordinate of the position vector is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-5t+6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t-3) & =0 \\\\ t & =2,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=2$ and $t=3.$" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349084 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349085 13194139535436 true] [17592186349084 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349086 13194139535436 true] [17592186349084 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535436 true] [17592186349084 1041 "field-1" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349084 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349085 13194139535436 true] [17592186349084 1110 #uuid "191fc677-f838-5a29-b4e3-bcd715b61adf" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349085 1034 "2" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349085 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535436 true] [17592186349085 1111 #uuid "93a22450-9b8a-51da-97d5-6f23d990ffae" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349086 1034 "1" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349086 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535436 true] [17592186349086 1111 #uuid "077d3234-8547-5bc6-a714-ee6d9fcb038b" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349087 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349088 13194139535436 true] [17592186349087 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535436 true] [17592186349087 1041 "field-2" 13194139535436 true] [17592186349087 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349088 13194